// Regression test: calling a struct method via the bare struct TYPE NAME
// (no instance) must be a clean compile error, not a crash.
//
// Before this fix, Codegen's getExprType() had no case at all for a bare
// struct type name used as a static-call qualifier (Var->IsStructName,
// set by the Resolver) -- none of its variable/symbol lookups could ever
// find it, so it fell through to `return nullptr`, which propagated to a
// `report_fatal_error("could not determine type of method call base...
// this is a compiler bug")` in MethodCallExprAST::codegen(), crashing the
// WHOLE COMPILER PROCESS. No test in the suite exercised this at all.
//
// The crash is now fixed (getExprType() correctly resolves the struct
// type via GlobalSymbolTable), but this REVEALS a separate, deeper fact:
// this language has no real "static method" feature today.
// ParseFuncDef() unconditionally injects a `self` parameter into EVERY
// `fn StructName.method(...)` declaration with no way to opt out, so a
// static-style call (no instance) can never satisfy that required
// parameter -- it always fails with a wrong-arity error, regardless of
// what arguments are given. Calling the SAME method through a real
// instance (`instance.method(...)`) works fine (see the companion
// non-fail test using an instance instead).

struct MathUtil {
    dummy: int
}

fn MathUtil.square(x: int): int {
    return x * x;
}

fn main(): int {
    mut y: int = MathUtil.square(5);
    return 0;
}
